export const DEVICE_KNOWLEDGE = "# RasQberry Two on this Pi — what you know\n\n## Summary (written at build time from the demo manifests, rq_help and the website; snapshot 2026-11-23)\n\nYou help someone sitting at a RasQberry Two: its browser opened rasqberry.org from the Pi (`?from=pi`). They may be the owner, a teacher, or a visitor at a stand. With each question you may get the Pi's facts in <page_state>: `device.version` (the image build), `device.model` (pi4 or pi5), `device.led` (the LED layout, `LED_LAYOUT`) and `device.ledcheck` (`LED_LAYOUT_VERIFIED`). Use them when they matter, e.g. Pi 4 vs Pi 5, a single vs a four-panel LED kit, or which demos and SSH/VNC rules this build has (see \"Setting up and fixing the Pi\"); don't recite them.\n\n### The LED layout comes first\n- `ledcheck` \"true\": the layout in `led` was checked on this panel. \"skipped\": the owner said there is no LED panel; LED demos can still show the panel on screen or in the browser (Output Targets). \"false\" or missing: nobody has checked yet, so `led` is only the default (`single-24x8`) and may be wrong for this kit. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_firstlogin.sh, rq_led_setup_wizard.sh, rq_display_ip.py]\n- When the check is not done and the question touches the LEDs in any way (dark, scrambled, upside down, which kit, a demo looks wrong), the layout is the likely answer: say so first and point to the check: desktop \"RasQberry Setup\" or `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"LED setup & tests\" → \"Check the LED Panel (which kit, which way up)\", or `sudo rq_led_setup_wizard.sh` for other panels and a wiring check. [development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh, RQB2-bin/rq_led_setup_wizard.sh]\n- Until the check is answered, the address scroll after boot alternates between the two kit layouts, so every second pass reads correctly on either kit. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_display_ip.py]\n\n### Starting a demo\n- Every demo has three ways in: the desktop folder of its group, the menu (`sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → the group), and the terminal (`rq_demo_run.sh <id>`, with a variant id where the demo has variants). The groups and demos are listed below. [development: RQB2-config/demo-manifests/demo-groups.json; RQB2-bin/rq_help]\n- Demos that are not on the image download on their first start, so that first start needs internet. Docker demos take 2–4 GB each. `rq_download_all.sh` downloads everything in advance (`--yes --docker` includes the Docker demos without asking), e.g. before an event without Wi-Fi; `rq_demo_remove.sh` (`--list` shows sizes) frees space again. [website: installation overview \"Which image?\"; development: RQB2-bin/rq_download_all.sh, rq_demo_remove.sh]\n- Whether a demo is on the image is stated per demo, per variant and per learning-path step below (\"on the image\" or \"downloads on first start\"). Variants of one demo can differ: Grokking the Bloch Sphere's `web` variant is on the image, the `local` variant used by the learning paths downloads at its first start. Never say a demo is on the image unless it says so below. [development: demo-manifests/rq_demo_grok-bloch.json, learning-paths.json]\n- **Which demos this Pi has depends on its image (demo levels, #371).** Each demo, variant and learning path below says where it ships (\"Ships in: …\"). Read `device.version`: `development-*` or `dev-*` = a development image (every demo); `beta-*` = a beta image: offer only demos, variants and learning-path steps that ship in beta images, never one for development images only. That holds for the published beta `beta-2026-11-15-225917`; `beta-2026-10-10-053754` and older betas are older than demo levels and have the demos of their time (Entangible and QuBins came later). Without a version, say that the demo may not be on every image. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_demo_level.py]\n- On development images, demos marked as development-level say \"(beta)\", sit in the \"Beta: try & tell us\" group and invite feedback through a GitHub issue form when they start. A demo with no level ships only in development images too, but has no mark and stays in its own group. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_demo_level.py; rq_common.sh rq_demo_maturity]\n\n### Choosing what to show\n- Learning path names differ by image (see \"Setting up and fixing the Pi\"): use the name this Pi shows.\n- More demos: Quantum Demos → Manage demos → \"Add demo from catalogue\". On `beta-2026-11-15-225917` and later betas it offers SAP Quantum Learning and SAP Quantum LED, racetraQ only on development images; older betas (`beta-2026-10-10-053754` and earlier) still offer racetraQ. [beta and beta-2026-10-10-053754: RQB2-config/known-demos.json]\n- For a first look, suggest the learning path \"First 15 minutes\" (Quantum Demos → Learning paths, or `rq_learning_paths.sh`); for a class, the longer paths. [development: demo-manifests/learning-paths.json]\n- At a stand, `sudo rq_demo_loop.sh` plays the demos that can run unattended one after another (`--choose` picks them). Only demos marked \"can run in the Demo Loop\" below take part. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; rq_demo_schema.json \"loop_ok\"]\n- No LED panel? LED demos can show the panel on screen or in the browser instead: LED panel → \"LED setup & tests\" → Output Targets. [development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n\n### When a demo doesn't work\n- A demo started from a desktop icon that fails keeps its window open; its output is also in `~/.cache/rasqberry/<demo>.log`. `rq_info.sh --report` collects the logs into a file for a bug report (https://github.com/JanLahmann/RasQberry-Two/issues). [development: RQB2-bin/rq_hold_on_error.sh, rq_info.sh]\n- \"GPIO busy\" or a dark panel: only one program can drive the LEDs. In the first minute after boot the panel shows the Pi's address; otherwise `sudo rq_clear_leds.sh --stop` frees it. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_clear_leds.sh; stage-RQB2/08-ip-display/README.md]\n- SSH or VNC from another device doesn't work: on `beta-2026-11-15-225917`, later betas and development images, with the published default password still set, SSH takes it only in the first 15 minutes after a start and VNC stays off until the password is changed (setup checklist on this Pi's screen). Details: https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/ → \"First access without a screen\". On older betas these rules don't exist; there VNC comes on only after the first desktop login. [beta and development: RQB2-bin/rq_ssh_window.sh]\n- Download fails: check the internet connection (`rq_info.sh` shows the address); guest Wi-Fi at events often blocks devices. If you don't know the cause, say so and suggest the report above.\n\n## Demo groups\n\n- LED panel: Demos on the LED panel, or in its on-screen view without a panel.\n- Play: Games and puzzles you play against or with a quantum computer.\n- Big projects: Larger builds that show what quantum computing can drive.\n- Learn & code: Write quantum programs and work through tutorials and courses.\n- Workshops & events: For a class or a stand: a loop of demos.\n- Contributed demos: Demos from other projects and partners. Add them with Manage demos > Add demo from catalogue.\n- Beta: try & tell us: New and experimental demos: some things may not work yet. Try them and tell us what works and what doesn't.\n\n## The demos (from the demo manifests on the Pi)\n\n### LED Demos — id `led-demos`, group \"LED panel\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nTests, text, logos and colour effects on the LED panel. Installed on the image.\nStart: `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"LED Demos\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh led-demos` <variant>.\nIt uses the LED panel, runs without a screen, no IBM Quantum account needed, can run in the Demo Loop.\nVariants: IBM Logo (`ibm-logo`, on the image, ships in beta and development images); Text Display (`text-display`, on the image, ships in beta and development images); Logo Display (`logo-display`, on the image, ships in beta and development images); LED Test (`led-test`, on the image, ships in beta and development images); Clear LEDs (`clear-leds`, on the image, ships in beta and development images).\n\n### RasQ-LED Demo — id `rasq-led`, group \"LED panel\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nCircuits with superposition and entanglement, measured and shown on the LED panel. Installed on the image.\nStart: desktop folder \"LED panel\" → \"RasQ-LED Demo\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"RasQ-LED Demo\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh rasq-led`.\nIt uses the LED panel, runs without a screen, no IBM Quantum account needed, can run in the Demo Loop.\n\n### Quantum Lights Out — id `quantum-lights-out`, group \"LED panel\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nWatch Grover's search solve Lights Out puzzles on the LED panel, one after another. Downloaded on first start (Lights Out solver from GitHub, about 1 MB, 10-30 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"LED panel\" → \"Quantum Lights Out\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"Quantum Lights Out\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-lights-out` <variant>.\nIt opens its own window, uses the LED panel, a screen is optional, no IBM Quantum account needed, can run in the Demo Loop.\nVariants: LED panel (`gui`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images); Console (`console`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images).\n\n### Quantum Raspberry Tie — id `quantum-raspberry-tie`, group \"LED panel\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nMeasured qubits on the LED panel, from a simulator or a real IBM quantum computer. Downloaded on first start (Raspberry Tie program from GitHub, about 24 MB, 10-30 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"LED panel\" → \"Quantum Raspberry Tie\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"Quantum Raspberry Tie\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-raspberry-tie` <variant>.\nIt opens its own window, uses the LED panel, needs a screen, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi), can run in the Demo Loop.\nVariants: Local simulator (no account needed) (`simulator`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images); Local simulator with a noise model (`noise`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images); Real IBM Quantum computer (IBM Quantum account) (`real`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only).\n\n### LED-Painter — id `led-painter`, group \"LED panel\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nPaint and draw on the LED panel from a graphical interface. Downloaded on first start (LED-Painter app from GitHub, about 2 MB, 10-30 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"LED panel\" → \"LED-Painter\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"LED-Painter\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh led-painter`.\nIt opens its own window, uses the LED panel, needs a screen, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### Fun with Quantum — id `fun-with-quantum`, group \"Play\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nQuantum games as Jupyter notebooks (Quantum Coin Game, GHZ game, Hardy's paradox, Mermin–Peres magic square, 3-SAT with Grover, CHSH game, quantum prisoner's dilemma, GHZ on real devices), the Fun with Quantum website with browser versions of the games (works offline), and the Fun with Quantum family of projects. Downloaded on first start (Jupyter notebooks and website from GitHub, about 30 MB, 10-30 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Play\" → \"Fun with Quantum\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Play → \"Fun with Quantum\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh fun-with-quantum` <variant>.\nIt opens as a Jupyter notebook in the browser, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\nVariants: Start here: what the games are about (`readme`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); Quantum Coin Game (`coin-game`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images); GHZ Game (`ghz-game`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); Hardy's Paradox (`hardys-paradox`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); Mermin–Peres Magic Square (`mermin-peres`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); 3-SAT with Grover's Algorithm (`3-sat`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); The CHSH Game (`chsh-game`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); The Quantum Prisoner's Dilemma (`prisoners-dilemma`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); GHZ on Real Quantum Devices (`ghz-real-devices`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); Fun with Quantum website: games in the browser (`website`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only); Fun with Quantum family: related projects (`family`, downloads on first start, ships in development images only).\n\n### Quantum Paradoxes — id `quantum-paradoxes`, group \"Play\"\nShips in: development images only.\nInteractive Jupyter notebooks exploring quantum paradoxes and phenomena. Downloaded on first start (Jupyter notebooks from GitHub, about 31 MB, 10-30 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Play\" → \"Quantum Paradoxes\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Play → \"Quantum Paradoxes\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-paradoxes`.\nIt opens as a Jupyter notebook in the browser, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n### Quantum Fractals — id `quantum-fractals`, group \"Play\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nJulia-set fractals that change with the state of one qubit, in the browser. Installed on the image.\nStart: desktop folder \"Play\" → \"Quantum Fractals\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Play → \"Quantum Fractals\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-fractals`.\nIt does not need the LED panel, needs a screen, no IBM Quantum account needed, can run in the Demo Loop.\n\n### Qoffee-Maker — id `qoffee-maker`, group \"Big projects\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nQuantum-controlled coffee maker using Home Connect API (Docker container). Downloaded on first start (Docker image from ghcr.io and the Qoffee-Maker notebooks, about 770 MB, 2-13 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Big projects\" → \"Qoffee-Maker\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Big projects → \"Qoffee-Maker\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh qoffee-maker`.\nIt runs in a Docker container, does not need the LED panel, needs a screen, needs internet while it runs, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### Quantum Mixer — id `quantum-mixer`, group \"Big projects\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nMix a cocktail, an ice cream or a coffee with a quantum circuit (Docker container). Downloaded on first start (Docker image built by the quantum-mixer repository's CI, about 900 MB, 2-15 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Big projects\" → \"Quantum Mixer\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Big projects → \"Quantum Mixer\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-mixer`.\nIt runs in a Docker container, does not need the LED panel, needs a screen, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### Entangible — id `entangible`, group \"Big projects\"\nShips in: development images only.\nBuild quantum circuits with printed gate tiles on a printed board: a camera reads them and the screen shows the circuit live; phones join over the network. Needs a USB webcam or the Pi camera. Downloaded on first start (Entangible from GitHub: its web app, a Python environment (OpenCV) and a system service, about 100 MB, 1-3 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Big projects\" → \"Entangible\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Big projects → \"Entangible\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh entangible`.\nIt does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### Grokking the Bloch Sphere — id `grok-bloch`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nInteractive Bloch sphere visualisation for understanding qubit states. Downloaded on first start (Bloch sphere web app from GitHub, about 6 MB, 15-90 seconds); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"Grokking the Bloch Sphere\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"Grokking the Bloch Sphere\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh grok-bloch` <variant>.\nIt opens in the browser, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, no IBM Quantum account needed.\nVariants: On this Pi (works offline, with tips) (`local`, downloads on first start, ships in beta and development images); Online version (javafxpert.github.io) (`web`, on the image, ships in beta and development images).\n\n### Grokking the Bloch Sphere (Web) — id `grok-bloch-web`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nInteractive 3D visualization and exploration of the Bloch sphere for quantum computing education (online version). Installed on the image.\nStart: terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh grok-bloch-web`.\nIt opens in the browser, does not need the LED panel, needs a screen, needs internet while it runs, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### doQumentation (IBM Quantum Learning on this Pi) — id `qiskit-tutorials`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: development images only.\nThe IBM Quantum tutorials, guides and courses as a website just for you, with live Qiskit code on this Pi. The Workshop & Qiskit Server for one person, without the network. Installed on the image.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"doQumentation (IBM Quantum Learning on this Pi)\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"doQumentation (IBM Quantum Learning on this Pi)\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh qiskit-tutorials`.\nIt does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n### Qiskit Live (QuBins) — id `quantum-lab`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: development images only.\nLocal JupyterLab quantum environment (QuBins signed community image) preloaded with the IBM Quantum Learning course notebooks (Docker container). Downloaded on first start (Docker image from ghcr.io (QuBins), and the IBM course notebooks if missing, about 890 MB, 2-15 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"Qiskit Live (QuBins)\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"Qiskit Live (QuBins)\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-lab`.\nIt runs in a Docker container, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, needs internet while it runs, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### QuBins — id `qubins`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: development images only.\nJupyterLab with the Qiskit version you choose (QuBins Docker images), and the notebooks you choose: IBM Quantum Tutorials and Courses, Quantum Paradoxes, Fun with Quantum, or only your own work. Downloaded on first start (A QuBins Docker image from ghcr.io (you choose which), and the notebooks you choose, about 890 MB, 2-15 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"QuBins\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"QuBins\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh qubins`.\nIt runs in a Docker container, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, needs internet while it runs, no IBM Quantum account needed.\n\n### IBM Quantum Tutorials — id `ibm-tutorials`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nOfficial IBM Quantum tutorials from Qiskit documentation (CC BY-SA 4.0). Downloaded on first start (IBM Quantum tutorials and courses from GitHub (Qiskit/documentation, CC BY-SA 4.0), about 80 MB, 1 minute); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"IBM Quantum Tutorials\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"IBM Quantum Tutorials\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh ibm-tutorials`.\nIt opens as a Jupyter notebook in the browser, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n### IBM Quantum Courses — id `ibm-courses`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nOfficial IBM Quantum learning courses from Qiskit documentation (CC BY-SA 4.0). Downloaded on first start (IBM Quantum tutorials and courses from GitHub (Qiskit/documentation, CC BY-SA 4.0), about 80 MB, 1 minute); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"IBM Quantum Courses\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"IBM Quantum Courses\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh ibm-courses`.\nIt opens as a Jupyter notebook in the browser, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n### IBM Quantum Composer — id `composer`, group \"Learn & code\"\nShips in: beta and development images.\nDesign and simulate quantum circuits in your web browser. Installed on the image.\nStart: desktop folder \"Learn & code\" → \"IBM Quantum Composer\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Learn & code → \"IBM Quantum Composer\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh composer`.\nIt opens in the browser, does not need the LED panel, needs a screen, needs internet while it runs, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n### Workshop & Qiskit Server — id `doqumentation`, group \"Workshops & events\"\nShips in: development images only.\nOne Pi serves the IBM Quantum tutorials, guides and courses to a group's laptops on the same network; code runs on this Pi. Built on doQumentation (Docker image, downloaded on first start). Downloaded on first start (Docker image from ghcr.io, about 1300 MB, 3-22 minutes); that needs internet once.\nStart: desktop folder \"Workshops & events\" → \"Workshop & Qiskit Server\"; `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → Workshops & events → \"Workshop & Qiskit Server\"; terminal: `rq_demo_run.sh doqumentation`.\nIt runs in a Docker container, does not need the LED panel, a screen is optional, needs internet while it runs, an IBM Quantum account is optional (used when one is saved on the Pi).\n\n## Learning paths (Quantum Demos → Learning paths, or `rq_learning_paths.sh`)\n\n### First 15 minutes — 15 minutes, for: Visitors at a stand\nShips in: beta and development images.\nGoal: Light up the panel, see a qubit and lose a coin game to a quantum computer.\n1. IBM LED Demo (demo `led-demos` variant `ibm-logo`, on the image): Look at the model: the IBM logo lights up on the LED panel. The LEDs are the model's display: other demos, like RasQ-LED, show qubits on them.\n2. Grokking the Bloch Sphere (demo `grok-bloch` variant `local`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Press H: the arrow moves from the top of the sphere to the equator. On the equator the qubit is in a superposition: 0 and 1 are equally likely.\n3. Quantum Coin Game (demo `fun-with-quantum` variant `coin-game`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Play heads or tails against a quantum computer: run each cell with Shift+Enter. The quantum computer wins every game. Its trick is the H gate you just saw.\nKeep going: path \"superposition-and-measurement\": Find out what the H gate did to the coin; Fun with Quantum https://fun-with-quantum.org: Play the quantum games at home, in your browser; QAMPoser https://qamposer.org: Build your own quantum circuits in the browser.\n\n### Superposition & measurement — 45 minutes, for: A school lesson\nShips in: beta and development images.\nGoal: Learn what a superposition is and what a measurement does to it.\n1. Grokking the Bloch Sphere (demo `grok-bloch` variant `local`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Press H: the arrow lies on the equator. Press H again: it is back at the top. The bar shows the chance of 0. A measurement gives 0 or 1, never both.\n2. Quantum Coin Game (demo `fun-with-quantum` variant `coin-game`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Play the classical game first, then the quantum one with the H gate. Two H gates bring the coin back to heads, whatever the other player did.\n3. Hardy's Paradox (demo `fun-with-quantum` variant `hardys-paradox`, downloads on first start, needs internet once; beta images leave this step out): Follow the quantum cars and run each cell with Shift+Enter. Everyday logic fails: you cannot reason about a measurement that did not happen.\nKeep going: Quantum mechanics module https://doqumentation.org/learning/modules/quantum-mechanics: Lessons on superposition, measurement and uncertainty, with Qiskit code; Basics of quantum information https://doqumentation.org/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information: The full course: qubits, measurements and gates, at your own pace; path \"entanglement\" (not on beta images): See what happens when two or three qubits share one state.\n\n### Entanglement — 40 minutes, for: Curious learners\nShips in: development images only.\nGoal: Win games that no classical team can win every time, with entangled qubits.\n1. GHZ Game (demo `fun-with-quantum` variant `ghz-game`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Look for a strategy that always wins, then let the quantum team play. No classical strategy wins every round. Three entangled qubits do.\n2. Mermin–Peres Magic Square (demo `fun-with-quantum` variant `mermin-peres`, downloads on first start, needs internet once): Look for a magic square that works, then run the quantum strategy for many rounds. Alice's and Bob's answers are random, yet they always agree where they meet.\n3. RasQ-LED Demo (demo `rasq-led`, on the image): Just watch: it entangles blocks of 1, 2, 3 ... qubits, up to the whole panel, and measures. Block size 1: random colours. In an entangled block every LED shows the same: red or blue.\nKeep going: CHSH inequality tutorial https://doqumentation.org/tutorials/chsh-inequality: Run a Bell test on a real quantum computer; Entanglement in action https://doqumentation.org/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/entanglement-in-action/introduction: Teleportation, superdense coding and the CHSH game, step by step; path \"your-first-program\": Write your own entangled circuit with Qiskit.\n\n### Your first program — 60 minutes, for: Learners who want to code\nShips in: every image.\nGoal: Write and run a quantum circuit with Qiskit, then keep learning.\n1. My Quantum Programs (`rq_my_programs.sh`): Hello-World.ipynb opens: run its cells one by one with Shift+Enter. Two entangled qubits: you measure 00 or 11, never 01 or 10.\n2. doQumentation (IBM Quantum Learning on this Pi) (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; beta images leave this step out; stable images leave this step out): Open Tutorials, then Hello World, and click Run All: the code runs on this Pi. The circuit from step 1, explained step by step, with more tutorials and courses next to it.\n3. IBM Quantum Learning (https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning): Take a free course, for example Basics of quantum information. It needs the internet. With an IBM Quantum account your code runs on real quantum computers.\nKeep going: Qiskit 101 hands-on https://doqumentation.org/workshop/Qiskit%20101%20Hands-on: A workshop notebook: your first circuits, a quiz and a run on real hardware; Fundamentals of quantum algorithms https://doqumentation.org/learning/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms: Query algorithms, phase estimation, factoring and Grover's search; CertiQ https://certiq.dev: Free community prep for the Qiskit developer certification (unofficial, not by IBM).\n\n### Introduction to quantum, adapted — 1560 minutes, for: Newcomers with time to learn\nShips in: every image.\nGoal: Learn key terms, applications and starter skills, on the Pi where you can.\n1. Quickstart (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/quick-start; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/quick-start): Build the circuit and run it (on this Pi, Qiskit is already installed). A few lines of code build a circuit, draw it and show its results.\n2. Run your first circuit on hardware (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/hello-world; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/hello-world): Follow its four steps: map, optimize, execute, analyze. Hardware needs an IBM Quantum account. The same four steps run every Qiskit program, from two qubits to over a hundred.\n3. IBM Quantum Composer (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/composer; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/composer): Read the guide, then build a circuit in the Composer (online, IBM Quantum account). The Composer draws circuits by drag and drop and shows their Qiskit code.\n4. Quantum computing in practice (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice): Seven lessons: start with the introduction, one lesson at a time. What quantum computers can do today, and how a problem gets onto one.\n5. Basics of quantum information (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information): Start with Single systems. Take your time: each lesson builds on the last. The ideas behind every other step: qubits, measurements, gates, entanglement.\n6. CHSH inequality (demo `qiskit-tutorials`, on the image; on beta images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/tutorials/chsh-inequality; on stable images this step opens https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/tutorials/chsh-inequality): Run it on real hardware with an IBM Quantum account. Entangled qubits break a limit no classical system can. It fails in Simulator mode for now.\nKeep going: Introduction to quantum at IBM https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en?modal=introduction-to-quantum: The same path online: earn its badge with an IBM Quantum account; path \"your-first-program\": Write your own circuits on this Pi.\n\n## Setting up and fixing the Pi\n\n### Summary (written at build time from the website, the development branch and the 3D-model branch)\n\nSnapshot 2026-11-23. Tags: [website: …] = rasqberry.org (gh-pages), [development: …] = branch `development` (VERSION `development-2026-11-23-220515`), [beta: …] = tag `beta-2026-11-15-225917`, [3D-model: …] = branch `3D-model`, all in github.com/JanLahmann/RasQberry-Two.\n\n**The published beta is `beta-2026-11-15-225917`** (in Imager since 2026-11-23); cards written from Imager's \"RasQberry Two Beta\" get it. The development branch is ahead of it: a [development] fact may not be in the beta yet. Where they differ, give the beta's behavior and say that development images may differ. Where a website page below says otherwise about this beta, the facts here are checked against the beta's code and win. **\"Older betas\"** below means `beta-2026-10-10-053754` and earlier, which many cards still run. A Pi's own build is in `device.version` when it asks from the Pi, else `cat /etc/rasqberry-version` or System Info.\n\n#### 1. What RasQberry Two is\n\nRasQberry Two is a functional model of IBM Quantum System Two: a 3D-printed model with a Raspberry Pi inside that runs Qiskit, quantum demos and games and drives an LED panel in the model's wall. Software, 3D files (Apache License 2.0 [3D-model: LICENSE]) and the assembly guide are open source. Hardware comes in two tiers: **Core** (the parts for the basic model, cheap and easy to buy) and **Optional** (UPS with 18650 batteries, power switch, LED ring for the cryostat, weights). No soldering is needed, and the guide assumes little or no hardware experience. Raspberry Pi 5 recommended, Pi 4B possible; the software is an SD-card image written with Raspberry Pi Imager. It is meant for teaching, meetups, classrooms and demo booths. Every demo also runs without an IBM Quantum account, on the built-in simulator. The System One predecessor is at rasqberry.one.\n\n#### 2. Where to find what\n\nMost of these pages are also in your context, word for word (below); the learning paths are linked only. This table says which page and section answers which question.\n\n| Question | Page → sections |\n|---|---|\n| Quick start | https://rasqberry.org/ → \"Getting started\" |\n| First start, setup checklist | https://rasqberry.org/02-software/01-installation-overview/ → \"First start\", \"Setup checklist\" |\n| Parts, prices, links, Core vs Optional, Pi/RAM, card, power supply, filament colors and grams, magnets, screws | https://rasqberry.org/01-3d-model/01-bill-of-materials/ → \"Required Components\", \"Core Components BOM\", \"Optional Components BOM\" |\n| STL location, 3D viewer, cooler, gluing, LED wiring and panel orientation, welding-shield filter (240 × 83 mm), Pi mounting, floor, cryostat/RTEs/door, magnet tips | https://rasqberry.org/01-3d-model/02-hardware-assembly-guide/ → \"Where to find the 3D files\", \"Explore the model in 3D\", \"Mounting the fan\", \"Gluing all the pieces together\", \"Wiring up the LED Arrays\", \"Cutting the welding shield\", \"Attach the Pi to the Wall\", \"Assembly of the Floor\", \"Assembly of the Cryostat\" |\n| Writing the image, Imager link and customization, which image, card sizes, default login, downloads, 0 RasQberry menu, versions, venv, updates, troubleshooting | https://rasqberry.org/02-software/01-installation-overview/ → \"Write the card\", \"Customisation\", \"If the link does not open Imager\", \"Which image?\", \"First start\", \"The RasQberry menu\" (also System Info for versions), \"Downloads\", \"Keeping up to date\", \"Troubleshooting\" |\n| Wi-Fi, setup Wi-Fi, Wi-Fi hotspot, SSH, VNC, sound, password, first access without a screen (the 15-minute SSH window) | https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/ → \"Wi-Fi\", \"Wi-Fi hotspot\", \"First access without a screen\", \"Sound\", \"Password\" |\n| A/B image: card sizes, update, what is kept, going back, Pi 4 bootloader | https://rasqberry.org/02-software/03-ab-boot/ → \"Card sizes\", \"Update\", \"What an update keeps\", \"Go back\", \"Raspberry Pi 4\" |\n| LED wizard, LED settings, LED troubleshooting, LED Python API | https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/led-display/ → \"What you can do\", \"Set up your panel first\", \"Configuration\", \"Troubleshooting\", \"For developers\" |\n| Running/stopping demos, no LED panel (on-screen/browser view), your own Qiskit programs, booth tips | https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/00-overview/ → \"Running demos\", \"No LED panel?\", \"Your own Qiskit programs\", \"At a booth or in class\" |\n| Demo list and ids; guided tours | https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/ ; https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/02-learning-paths/ |\n| Standalone (floorless) model, printed LED filter, 8×32 panel cut to 8×24, beta tips, update from a branch, building images | https://rasqberry.org/04-jans-corner/tips-and-tricks/ |\n| Workshops, one Pi as class server; contributing | https://rasqberry.org/workshops/ ; https://rasqberry.org/05-contributing/ |\n\n#### 3. Facts from the implementation\n\n##### Hardware and images\n- RasQberry images in the Imager list are for `pi5-64bit` and `pi4-64bit` only. The Imager list also offers plain \"Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit) — without RasQberry\", which also lists Pi 3. [website: public/RQB-images.json]\n- The model is detected at every boot, and an SD card can move between a Pi 4 and a Pi 5. The LED driver differs: PWM on the Pi 4, PIO (RP1) on the Pi 5. An unknown model is treated as a Pi 5. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_detect_hardware.sh; stage-RQB2/02-system-integration/README.md]\n- In Imager the current beta is \"RasQberry Two Beta\" (the A/B image, recommended) and \"RasQberry Two Beta — single system\", both `beta-2026-11-15-225917`. The A/B download is about 1.8 GB (12.4 GB unpacked). Dev and branch builds are under \"RasQberry developer builds\": \"RasQberry Two Dev A/B (development …)\" and \"RasQberry Two Dev (development …)\" with the newest date are the development images; entries with another name in the parentheses are experiments. Don't promise a date for the next beta. [website: public/RQB-images.json]\n- A downloaded image written with Imager's **Choose OS → Use custom** gets **no customization** (no Wi-Fi, user, password or SSH key from Imager). For customization, use the rasqberry.org repository link in Imager (Content Repository → Use custom URL), as the installation page shows. [website: 02-software/01-installation-overview]\n- Release streams are dev < beta < stable. Tags `beta-*` are beta, `development-*`/`dev-*` are dev, and `v1.2.3`/`stable-*` are stable. No stable release exists yet. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_update_check.sh; website: public/RQB-releases.json]\n- The published beta and the development branch run Raspberry Pi OS **Trixie** (Debian 13, 64-bit, Python 3.13), as the website says. Older betas, up to `beta-2026-10-06-211827`, are **Bookworm** (Python 3.11); Trixie starts with `beta-2026-10-07-213933`. When it matters, check `device.version`, System Info or `rq_info.sh`. [development: pi-gen-config at each beta tag; website: installation overview \"The RasQberry menu\"]\n- **Demo levels (#371, in the published beta and development):** an image ships only the demos of its level. Development images have every demo, beta images the demos marked for beta or for every image, stable images only those for every image. The demo list for the Pi says per demo \"Ships in: …\". In the published beta, these are **development images only**: the demos Workshop & Qiskit Server, doQumentation (IBM Quantum Learning on this Pi), Qiskit Live (QuBins), QuBins, Quantum Paradoxes and Entangible (the website's demo list marks them \"dev image\"); of Fun with Quantum, every variant except the Quantum Coin Game (`coin-game`), so the CHSH, GHZ, Hardy, Mermin–Peres, 3-SAT and prisoner's dilemma notebooks and the offline website are not on the beta; and Quantum Raspberry Tie's `real` variant (IBM hardware). The per-variant \"Ships in\" in the demo list for the Pi is complete. On development images, demos marked as development-level show \"(beta)\" and sit in the \"Beta: try & tell us\" group; a demo with no level at all also ships only in development images but has no mark. Beta images have no such group. Older betas are older than demo levels and have the demos of their time: Entangible and QuBins came later. [beta and development: RQB2-bin/rq_demo_level.py, demo-manifests; website: demo list]\n- **Learning path names:** the beta image calls one path \"IBM: Introduction to quantum\"; development images and the website call the same path \"Introduction to quantum, adapted\" (based on IBM Quantum Learning's path, with RasQberry's changes). Same path, same id `ibm-introduction-to-quantum`. [beta and development: demo-manifests/learning-paths.json]\n\n##### First boot\n- On the standard image the root partition grows to fill the card at the first boot, followed by a restart. An empty file `skip-expansion` on the boot partition prevents this. [development: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md]\n- On the A/B image `rasqberry-ab-layout.service` sets up the card instead. On a card of 58 GiB or more (\"64 GB\" ≈ 59.6 GiB) it makes two systems: 45 % Slot A, 45 % Slot B, 10 % DATA. A smaller card gets one system. An empty `no-auto-expand` (or `no-auto-expand.txt`) on the CONFIG partition keeps the card as written. If the power is cut during this step, the next start finishes it. Log: `/var/log/rasqberry-expand.log`. A small card cannot become a two-system card later. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Imager customization on an A/B card adds an extra restart. Imager writes it to CONFIG, the Pi moves it to BOOT-A and restarts, then applies it and restarts again. It applies only on the first start of a newly written card. [development: docs/ab-boot.md; stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md]\n- A user name typed in Imager becomes the login (home `/home/<name>`; #319, in every build since 2026-10-07, so also in the published beta). If the name cannot be used, the user stays `rasqberry` and the first login says why. Log: `/var/log/rasqberry-user-rename.log`. The website says the same. [development: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md; website: installation overview \"Customisation\"]\n- **First access without a screen** (published beta and development; the website explains it under System Options → \"First access without a screen\"). With a password set in Imager, SSH and VNC use it, and VNC is on from the first start. Without a user or SSH key from Imager, the Pi keeps the published default password `Qiskit1!` but protects it; mention that password only as this documented first-access default that must be changed, as the website does:\n  - SSH accepts it only in the first 15 minutes after each start, counted from when the Pi is on the network. The first SSH login must change it: first the old password, then the new one twice. The new password is also the VNC and `sudo` password. Sessions opened in the 15 minutes keep running; key logins are never affected.\n  - After those 15 minutes, SSH takes keys only and shows a banner: restart the Pi and log in within 15 minutes, or set a password or SSH key in Imager.\n  - VNC stays off until the password is changed, then comes on once. A keys-only card from Imager that still has the published password waits for VNC the same way.\n  - With a screen: change the password in the setup checklist (or Remote Access & Security, or `passwd`).\n  - Booths and classrooms can change both in `rasqberry_boot.env` on the boot drive (bootfs; BOOT-A or BOOT-B on the A/B image: remove the `#` in front of the line), read at every start: `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_MIN` (minutes, default 15; `0` = keys only) and `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_VNC=yes` (VNC on before the change).\n  - Once the password is changed, these rules go away. An A/B update keeps a card's VNC state: on stays on, off stays off.\n  [website: 02-software/02-system-options; beta and development: RQB2-bin/rq_ssh_window.sh, rq_ssh_first_login.sh, rq_carry_over.sh; stage-RQB2/10-boot-config/files/rasqberry_boot.env]\n- **Older betas** (`beta-2026-10-10-053754` and earlier) don't have these rules: there VNC is switched on once at the first desktop login, and SSH and VNC take the password at any time. Advise such cards to update (A/B: Software & Image Updates) or to change the password. [beta-2026-10-10-053754: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md]\n- **Setup hotspot (new Pi without a network):** a card written without Wi-Fi and started without a cable opens, after about two minutes with no network, the Wi-Fi \"RasQberry-Setup-XXXX\" (password `rasqberry`, documented). Joining it opens a page that lists the Wi-Fi networks in range; the Pi joins the one chosen, and the LED panel then scrolls its new address. It opens only while the Pi has never had a network, and ends when there is a network, after 30 minutes with nobody on it, or after an hour at most. Whether devices on it may use SSH and VNC follows the hotspot's setting below. [beta: RQB2-bin/rq_setup_hotspot.sh; docs/release-notes-next.md]\n- The setup checklist (`rq_firstlogin.sh`) opens by itself at desktop and SSH logins until someone answers it (Run, Later or Esc). After that it opens from the \"RasQberry Setup\" desktop icon or the menu. In current code the icon disappears once nothing is pending or \"Don't show again\" is ticked. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_firstlogin.sh]\n- For about 60 s after boot the LED panel scrolls the Pi's name and IP address, and it scrolls them again when they change. Until the LED check is answered, the passes alternate between the two kit layouts, so every second pass is readable on either kit. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_display_ip.py; stage-RQB2/08-ip-display/README.md]\n- A Wi-Fi watchdog (from 90 s after boot, then every minute) reconnects saved Wi-Fi profiles that NetworkManager gave up on, e.g. when the router refused the first attempts at a headless first start. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_wifi_watchdog.sh]\n- A health check (`rasqberry-health-check.service`, `rq_health_check.py`) runs at every boot. It checks that the Python venv exists and Qiskit is installed, and on a trial A/B boot that the display manager came up. On a trial boot it confirms the slot or rolls back. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_health_check.py; docs/ab-boot.md]\n- At start-up the Pi checks its bootloader EEPROM. If the EEPROM is older than about six months and a newer one exists, or the Pi 5 crypto service is missing, the desktop says so once and suggests `sudo rpi-eeprom-update -a` and a restart. RasQberry never updates firmware itself. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_firmware.py; docs/release-notes-next.md]\n\n##### New in the published beta (`beta-2026-11-15-225917`, compared with `beta-2026-10-10-053754`)\n- **Wi-Fi hotspot for booths and classrooms:** `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → \"Wi-Fi Hotspot (booths and classrooms)\". Phones and laptops join the Pi's own Wi-Fi and reach the demos at http://10.42.0.1. Entries: Hotspot on/off; \"Show name, password and QR codes\" (1. join the Wi-Fi, 2. open the demos page); \"Join card on the screen (to show or print)\"; \"Change name and password\"; \"Start at boot\"; \"Share this Pi's internet (cable or USB Wi-Fi)\"; Band (5 GHz by default, or 2.4 GHz); \"SSH/VNC from the hotspot\": auto (recommended: only once the default password is changed), no, or yes. The hotspot uses a USB Wi-Fi adapter if there is one; otherwise the built-in Wi-Fi leaves its network while the hotspot runs, so SSH, VNC or Connect over that Wi-Fi ends (a network cable keeps the Pi online). Before the first start, a file `rasqberry-hotspot.txt` on the boot partition (CONFIG on an A/B card) with lines such as `HOTSPOT_SSID=…`, `HOTSPOT_PASSWORD=…`, `HOTSPOT_AT_BOOT=yes` sets the hotspot up; the Pi saves the settings and keeps no password in the file, and lists what it didn't take in `rasqberry-hotspot.applied.txt`. The website explains it under System Options → \"Wi-Fi hotspot\". [beta: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh, RQB2-bin/rq_hotspot.sh; website: 02-software/02-system-options]\n- **Demo catalogue, now by level:** Quantum Demos → Manage demos → \"Add demo from catalogue\" (also `rq_demo_add_external.sh --list`) was already in the older beta, with racetraQ, SAP Quantum Learning and SAP Quantum LED. New: an image offers only the catalogue demos of its level, so the published beta offers the two SAP demos (provided by SAP), and racetraQ (the Fun with Quantum family) is offered on development images only; a card on an older beta still offers racetraQ. [beta, beta-2026-10-10-053754 and development: RQB2-config/known-demos.json; RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n- **IBM Quantum Learning paths:** the learning path \"IBM: Introduction to quantum\" (on development images \"Introduction to quantum, adapted\") links IBM Quantum Platform guides and Learning courses. [beta: demo-manifests/learning-paths.json]\n- **Ket on the Pi:** the Pi's browser start page is rasqberry.org opened from the Pi, with the Ket chat button. [beta: RQB2-bin/rq_desktop_session.py]\n- **Bluetooth sound delay:** Desktop Settings (touch mode, browser at login, sound delay) → \"Sound delay (Bluetooth speaker)\": how long the LEDs of demos with sound wait for the speaker (Bluetooth speakers play 100–300 ms late). [beta: docs/reference/menu.md]\n- **LED demos run in a sandbox:** LED demos, catalogue LED demos included, run as the normal user in an LED sandbox, not as root. [beta: RQB2-bin/rq_demo_add_external.sh, rq_audio_delay_calibrate.sh]\n- **Qiskit 2.6** in the Python environment (`~/RasQberry-Two/venv/RQB2`): the beta image has Qiskit 2.6.2 and Aer 0.18.2. A Pi shows its versions (image, Python, Qiskit) under 0 RasQberry → System Info. [website: home page beta box, installation overview \"The RasQberry menu\"; RasQberry team, checked on the beta image 2026-11-24]\n\n##### LED panel\n- Supported layouts (`LED_LAYOUT`): `single-24x8`: the default, one 24×8 panel, 192 LEDs; `quad-4x12`: four 4×12 panels, two above two, as mounted in the model; `quad-2x2-12x4`: the legacy quad mapping; `triple-8x8`: three 8×8 panels side by side; `single-8x32`: one uncut 32×8 panel, 256 LEDs; Custom layouts go in `~/.local/config/led-layouts.json`. [development: RQB2-config/led-layouts.json; RQB2-config/CONFIG_FILES.md]\n- The LED Setup Wizard (`sudo rq_led_setup_wizard.sh`) shows the IBM logo as single-24x8 in **blue, steady**, then as quad-4x12 in **yellow, blinking** (chosen for red-green color blindness); if neither reads upright, both upside down; then a per-panel walkthrough. Each answer is confirmed with a white logo before saving. \"Nothing lights up / no panel\" gives a wiring and power checklist and can save `LED_LAYOUT_VERIFIED=skipped`. A \"diagnostic only\" mode saves nothing; `--verify` is the short check of the setup checklist. Three 8×8 panels count as a single panel. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_led_setup_wizard.sh]\n- Menu: `sudo raspi-config` → 0 RasQberry → Quantum Demos → LED panel → \"LED setup & tests (brightness, check, wizard)\" (title \"RasQberry: LEDs\"): Turn off all LEDs; LED brightness (weak power supply?); Text & Logo Display; Quick LED Test (6 colours); LED Test & Diagnostics; Simple LED Demo; IBM LED Demo; Check the LED Panel (which kit, which way up); Output Targets (panel / on-screen / browser); LED Setup Wizard (other panels, wiring check). [development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n- Brightness levels (`rq_led_brightness.sh`, or `--set low|medium|normal|bright`): Low 0.2 (cap 0.2), for a weak power supply; Medium 0.3 (cap 0.3); Normal 0.4 (as shipped, cap 1.0); Bright 0.6 (cap 1.0), needs the 27 W supply; The setting writes `LED_DEFAULT_BRIGHTNESS` and `LED_MAX_BRIGHTNESS`. After an LED stall it explains the likely cause from what the Pi reported (power or heat). It offers a lower level only when power was short. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_led_brightness.sh]\n- Output Targets defaults: panel on (`LED_PHYSICAL=true`), on-screen view on (`LED_VIRTUAL=true`), browser view off (`LED_WEB=false`, port 8098). At least one stays on. [development: RQB2-config/rasqberry_environment.env; RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n- **LED settings before the first start:** the boot drive (\"bootfs\" on the standard image, BOOT-A on the A/B image) has `rasqberry_boot.env`. Uncomment lines such as `LED_LAYOUT=quad-4x12` to preset a whole class set. These values are applied at **every** start and override changes made later in the menu. Invalid values are skipped. Log: `journalctl -u rasqberry-boot-config -b`. [development: stage-RQB2/10-boot-config/README.md; stage-RQB2/10-boot-config/files/rasqberry_boot.env]\n- Test commands: `sudo rq_led_test.sh` (interactive test); `rq_demo_run.sh led-demos led-test`; `rq_demo_run.sh led-demos` without a part lists the LED demo parts: `ibm-logo`, `text-display`, `logo-display`, `led-test`, `clear-leds`; `sudo rq_clear_leds.sh` (`--stop` also stops the program holding the panel, `--holders` lists such programs) [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; RQB2-config/demo-manifests/rq_demo_led-demos.json; RQB2-bin/rq_clear_leds.sh; docs/release-notes-next.md]\n- Own LED programs: on a Pi 5, `rq_python` drives the panel as the normal user. On a Pi 4 the PWM driver needs root, so `rq_python` starts a root LED renderer service and runs the program as the user. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_python]\n- On an A/B card the LED settings are also kept on `/data`, so they survive an update into the other slot. [development: RQB2-config/CONFIG_FILES.md]\n\n##### Commands a builder would type (`rq_help` lists them)\n- Menu and setup: `sudo raspi-config`: the menu, including 0 RasQberry; `rq_firstlogin.sh --all`: the setup checklist; `rq_info.sh`: name, address, power and version; `rq_info.sh --report`: saves `~/rasqberry-report-<date>.txt` with logs for a bug report; `rq_info.sh --json`: build data as JSON; `rq_remote_access.sh status`, `sudo rq_remote_access.sh ssh on|off`, `vnc on|off`, `name NEW`; `passwd` [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; RQB2-bin/rq_info.sh]\n- Demos: `rq_demo_run.sh <demo>`; `rq_learning_paths.sh`; `rq_download_all.sh` (`--yes --docker` downloads the Docker demos too, without asking); `rq_demo_remove.sh` (`--list` shows sizes); `sudo rq_demo_loop.sh` (`--choose` picks the demos); `rq_demo_add_external.sh --list` [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; rq_download_all.sh; rq_demo_remove.sh; rq_demo_loop.sh]\n- Programming: `rq_python program.py`: the venv with Qiskit and the LED helpers. Never `sudo python3`; `rq_my_programs.sh`: JupyterLab in `~/My-Quantum-Programs`; `rq_venv_repair.sh`: reports only. `--fix-ownership` fixes root-owned files. `--reset` restores a fresh venv and keeps the old one as `<venv>.previous`. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; RQB2-bin/rq_venv_repair.sh]\n- System: `rq_update_check.sh`: exit 0 means up to date, 10 means a newer image exists; `rq_touch_mode.sh status|enable|disable`; `sudo rq_slot_manager.sh status`; `sudo rq_after_update.sh status`; `sudo rq_expand_ab.sh status`; `cat /etc/rasqberry-version`: this slot's build. Do not use `/etc/rpi-issue` for that; `sudo poweroff` [development: RQB2-bin/rq_help; RQB2-bin/rq_update_check.sh; docs/ab-boot.md]\n- `sudo rq_update_from_branch.sh --branch <b> [--repo user/repo] [--dry-run]`, `--restore`: updates scripts and configs only. It does not update packages, the kernel or the venv. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_update_from_branch.sh]\n\n##### raspi-config \"0 RasQberry\" (labels as in the code)\n- Software & Image Updates: \"Check for a newer image\", plus one entry that depends on the card: two systems: \"Slot Manager (install updates, switch systems)\"; A/B card not set up yet: \"Prepare the card for A/B updates (64 GB or larger card)\" or \"Use the whole card (one system)\"; A/B image on a small card: \"Why there are no A/B updates on this card\" [development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n- Slot Manager: \"Install an update into the other system (Slot X)\", \"Switch to Slot X (restart and try it)\", \"Show slot details\", plus confirm/rollback entries when they apply. [development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh]\n- The menu is added by patching raspi-config at every boot (`rq_patch_raspiconfig.sh`, root cron `@reboot`). After an apt upgrade of raspi-config it is back after a restart. [development: stage-RQB2/02-system-integration/README.md]\n\n##### Updates (A/B)\n- An update goes into the slot that is not running. The new slot is tried once (\"on probation\") and becomes the start slot only after the health check confirms it. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Automatic rollback: A kernel that cannot start reboots after 10 s (`panic=10`); A failed check reboots into the old slot; An unconfirmed trial is rolled back after 15 minutes. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Space: the download is checked against its SHA256, then unpacked straight into the other slot. It needs the download size plus 0.5 GB free; the preflight asks for 3.0 GB. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Guards: A warning before a downgrade; A typed `REPLACE` before overwriting the only beta or stable system; A typed `RASQBERRY` before installing a release older than the user-name feature when the user has another name. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Kept across updates: On DATA: `~/Shared`, `~/My-Quantum-Programs`, `~/.qiskit`, Wi-Fi networks and LED settings; Copied once: password hash, hostname, time zone, locale, keyboard, Raspberry Pi Connect sign-in, SSH host keys and `authorized_keys`; from the published beta on also whether VNC is on or off [beta and development: RQB2-bin/rq_carry_over.sh]; Not kept: installed demos, Docker images, added Python packages and other home files. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Your own packages after an update: root-owned executable scripts in `/data/rasqberry/after-update.d/` run once per release after confirmation. Log: `/var/log/rasqberry/after-update.log`. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Taskbar badge: green = confirmed start slot; amber = trial start or the other slot starts next; red \"!\" = the last update or switch failed and the Pi went back; blue dot = newer release. On the standard image a grey \"Q\" badge appears only while a newer release exists. Betas are announced 3 days after release (stable 7), in a staged rollout. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n- Command-line update: `sudo rq_update_slot.sh --preflight`, `rq_ab_releases.sh latest`, `sudo rq_update_slot.sh <ab-image-url> <release-tag>`. Only the `-ab.img.xz` image can fill a slot. Exit codes 20–29 name the reason for a refusal; e.g. 22 means not enough space and 21 means the card is not prepared or has one system only. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n\n##### Logs and diagnostics\n- When a demo started from a desktop icon fails, its window stays open. A copy of the output is in `~/.cache/rasqberry/<demo>.log`, and `rq_info.sh --report` collects it. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_hold_on_error.sh; RQB2-bin/rq_common.sh]\n- Other logs: `journalctl -u rasqberry-firstboot.service`, `journalctl -u rasqberry-ab-layout`, `/var/log/rasqberry-expand.log`, `/var/log/rasqberry-imager.log`, `journalctl -u rasqberry-boot-config -b`. [development: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md; stage-RQB2/10-boot-config/README.md]\n\n##### Usage statistics\n- The Pi sends anonymous counts to the project's Umami statistics: first start, daily update check, update result, update-notice clicks, demo starts, learning paths and LED stalls. It never sends a serial number, hostname, IP or user name. [development: docs/ab-boot.md]\n\n#### 4. 3D printing\n\n- **Folders** in \"3D Model/\" on the 3D-model branch: `wall/`: R2_Wall-Base, R2_Wall-Back, R2_Wall-Lid, R2_Screw, R2_Hex_Nut, and two Back variants, `R2_Wall-Back+13` and `R2_Wall-Back-SolidForStaticModels`. The sources do not explain the variants; `floor/`: R2_Floor-Tile1 to Tile5, Tile6s, R2_Floor-all, and \"Sign Magnet Indent Version\" (Tile1Mag, Tile2Mag); `cryostat/`: R2_NarrowYCryoConjoined, R2_CryoLid-Simple, R2_ChandelierSingle, R2_NewDoor, R2_ScrewOctopusAlignment2, R2_NarrowServerSingleLid, and \"Standalone Version\" (R2_CryoStandaloneBeta, R2_CryoStandaloneGamma, R2_ServerStandalone, R2_NarrowServerSingle); `RTE/`: R2_NarrowServerSingle, R2_NarrowServerSingleLid; `union/`: empty; Most parts exist twice: the original and `*_repaired.stl`, auto-fixed by a mesh-repair workflow. Print the `_repaired` versions. [3D-model: 3D Model/; README.md; .github/scripts/stl_analyzer.py]\n- **File types:** only STL, 67 files. There are no parametric or CAD source files (no STEP, SCAD or F3D). [3D-model: file listing]\n- **Jan's modifications:** \"3D Model - modifications - Jan/\" has \"October 2025\" and \"October 2025 - no-floor\". It holds modified Wall-Base, a CryoStandaloneGamma variant, RQB2-Cryostat, an RTE single, and RQB2-WallPanel-jrl02-2.stl, a 0.6 mm printed LED filter with no `_repaired` copy. The website explains these as printer tweaks and the floorless standalone model. [3D-model: 3D Model/3D Model - modifications - Jan/; website: content/04-jans-corner/tips-and-tricks.md]\n- **Approximate part sizes**, read from the STL bounding boxes (STL units, assumed to be mm): Wall-Base and Wall-Lid: about 246 × 60 mm; Wall-Back: about 245 × 10 × 81 mm; Floor tiles: 130 × 130 × 10 mm each; R2_Floor-all: 260 × 390 mm in one piece, which needs a very large bed. Otherwise print the six tiles; Cryostat core (NarrowYCryoConjoined): about 157 × 136 × 93 mm; RTE single: about 38 × 23 × 93 mm; Printed filter panel: 240.5 × 91 × 0.6 mm; The wall parts need about 246 mm of bed in one direction. The guide mentions halves glued together, but the branch contains only one-piece wall STLs. The sources do not say how to split them. [3D-model: 3D Model/ (bounding boxes computed from the STL files)]\n- **Printing notes:** the parts print without supports [3D-model: README.md]. Door orientation, chandeliers, RTE count, filament and the printed filter are on the website (assembly guide, BOM, Jan's corner).\n\n#### 5. Troubleshooting\n\n- **LED panel dark, first minute after boot:** the IP-address scroll holds the LED GPIO for about 60 s, and LED demos started then report \"GPIO busy\". Wait, or use \"Stop a running LED demo\". [development: stage-RQB2/08-ip-display/README.md]\n- **LED panel dark, another program holds it:** only one process can drive the panel. `sudo rq_clear_leds.sh --holders` lists the holders and `--stop` frees the panel. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_clear_leds.sh; website: led-display \"Troubleshooting\"]\n- **LEDs stop after a while (Pi 5):** too little power or too much heat. Use the official 27 W supply, lower the brightness, and fit the Active Cooler. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_led_brightness.sh]\n- **Text or logos scrambled or upside down:** run the LED Setup Wizard so it sets `LED_LAYOUT`. On older images, text and logos read the retired `LED_MATRIX_LAYOUT` and came out scrambled on the four-panel kit. [website: led-display; development: RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh comments]\n- **LED layout comes back after reboot even though the wizard changed it:** check `rasqberry_boot.env` on the boot drive. Uncommented values there win at every start. [development: stage-RQB2/10-boot-config/files/rasqberry_boot.env]\n- **Wrong LED colors:** check `LED_PIXEL_ORDER`. The default is GRB. [development: RQB2-config/rasqberry_environment.env]\n- **LED wiring on old images:** images older than the 2025-12-30 beta used SPI and a different wiring. Current images use GPIO 18 on pin 12, 5 V on pin 2 and GND on pin 6. [website: hardware assembly guide]\n- **Wi-Fi offline after a headless first start:** the watchdog retries saved profiles every minute. By hand: `nmcli connection up preconfigured` (Imager's profile name). [development: RQB2-bin/rq_wifi_watchdog.sh]\n- **Pi not found on the network:** with several Pis named rasqberry, the later ones become `rasqberry-2.local`, `-3`, … in no fixed order. Use \"Name this RasQberry\" or `sudo rq_remote_access.sh name NEW`. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_remote_access.sh]\n- **A/B card still at about 10 GB / Slot B a 16 MB placeholder:** Cause: `no-auto-expand` on CONFIG, or a card written from an older image; Check: `sudo rq_expand_ab.sh status`; Fix: Software & Image Updates → \"Prepare the card for A/B updates\". [development: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md]\n- **Black screen after an update:** power cycle; the previous slot starts. If that fails, set `boot_partition=2` under `[all]` in `autoboot.txt` on CONFIG (3 for Slot B). [development: docs/ab-boot.md; website: ab-boot \"Go back\"]\n- **Updating from a round-3 beta A/B card:** the old update menu stops with a false \"corrupted\" error. Run from a terminal on Slot A: `sudo rq_update_slot.sh <URL of the -ab image> <release tag> --slot B > ~/update.log 2>&1`, or write a new card. [development: docs/release-notes-next.md]\n- **Firmware updates on an A/B card:** older code put an EEPROM update into the running slot, which can stop a Pi 4 from starting. Current code stages it on CONFIG. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_firmware.py; docs/release-notes-next.md]\n- **Raspberry Pi Connect from Imager does not sign in:** update the EEPROM (`sudo rpi-eeprom-update -a`, restart), then sign in from the taskbar. [development: docs/release-notes-next.md]\n- **0 RasQberry missing from raspi-config:** restart (the menu is re-patched at boot), or run `sudo /usr/bin/rq_patch_raspiconfig.sh`. [development: stage-RQB2/02-system-integration/README.md]\n- **Grokking the Bloch Sphere: the tips panel doesn't scroll on a small window** (known issue in `beta-2026-11-15-225917`, fixed on development): make the browser window larger or full screen. [RasQberry team, beta release notice 2026-11-23]\n- **Imager offers no customization:** the image was chosen with Choose OS → Use custom. Use the rasqberry.org repository link instead (installation page). [website: 02-software/01-installation-overview]\n- **Python environment broken** (\"Permission denied\" on pip, missing jupyter): `rq_venv_repair.sh --fix-ownership` or `--reset`. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_venv_repair.sh]\n- **VNC does not connect:** with the published default password still set, VNC is off on purpose: change the password (VNC then comes on), or set `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_VNC=yes` in `rasqberry_boot.env` and restart. On older betas, VNC comes on only after the first desktop login. Over the hotspot, check \"SSH/VNC from the hotspot\". Otherwise `sudo raspi-config nonint do_vnc 0`; check `systemctl status wayvnc.service`. [beta and development: RQB2-bin/rq_ssh_window.sh, RQB2-config/RQB2_menu.sh; beta-2026-10-10-053754: stage-RQB2/00-firstboot-setup/README.md]\n- **SSH refuses the password (published default password still set):** the 15 minutes after the start are over (the login banner says so). Restart the Pi and log in within 15 minutes (it then asks for a new password), log in with a key, or change the password at the desktop. If `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_MIN=0` is set in `rasqberry_boot.env`, there is no such time: only a key or the desktop. [development: RQB2-bin/rq_ssh_window.sh]\n\n#### 6. Not covered by the sources\n\nSay these are not covered instead of guessing:\n\n- Pi 3, Zero, 400/500, Compute Modules, USB/NVMe boot (the images list Pi 4 and Pi 5 only).\n- Print settings (layer height, infill, nozzle, temperatures, print times); filament per part (the BOM gives grams per color); bed size beyond the part sizes above.\n- How to split the wall parts for small printers; the purpose of `R2_Wall-Back+13`, `R2_Wall-Back-SolidForStaticModels` and the floor \"Sign Magnet Indent Version\".\n- Mounting and wiring of the optional parts (X1203 UPS, LED ring, power switch); any software for the LED ring.\n- Panels other than WS2812/WS2812B, other GPIO pins in practice, level shifters, and how to wire the separate 5 V supply the guide recommends for a brighter panel.\n- Tested touchscreen models; resin printing or laser cutting; prices beyond the BOM's December 2024 list.\n- When a stable release comes, and when the next beta is published. Which OS an older card has, if you don't know its build (check System Info or `rq_info.sh`).\n\n## Website pages (rasqberry.org, as published)\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/00-overview/\n\n# Quantum Computing Demos Overview\n\nDemos, games and notebooks that make superposition, interference and entanglement\nvisible, on screen and on the LED panel. Each one in detail: the\n[demo list](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/). Not sure where to start? Try a\n[learning path](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/02-learning-paths/).\n\n## The demos at a glance\n\nSix groups, each a folder on the desktop and a submenu of **Quantum Demos** in the\nRasQberry menu. *Dev* marks demos that are in the\n[development image](https://rasqberry.org/latest/dev/) only, not in the beta.\n\n| Group | What is in it |\n|-------|---------------|\n| **LED panel** | IBM LED Demo, RasQ-LED, [Quantum Lights Out](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/quantum-lights-out/), [Quantum Raspberry Tie](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/raspberry-tie/), LED-Painter, [text and logos](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/led-display/) |\n| **Play** | Quantum Coin Game (seven more Fun with Quantum games: *dev*), [Quantum Fractals](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/fractals/), Quantum Paradoxes (*dev*) |\n| **Big projects** | [Qoffee-Maker](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/qoffee-maker/), Quantum Mixer, [Entangible](#2-entangible) (*dev*), [racetraQ](https://racetraq.org) (*dev*) |\n| **Learn & code** | [My Quantum Programs](#2-your-own-qiskit-programs), [Grokking the Bloch Sphere](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/bloch-sphere/), IBM Quantum Tutorials and Courses, IBM Quantum Composer; doQumentation and Qiskit Live (*dev*) |\n| **Workshops & events** | Demo Loop; Workshop & Qiskit Server (*dev*) |\n| **Contributed demos** | Partner demos from the catalog: SAP Quantum LED, SAP Quantum Learning |\n\nOnly the LED Demos (text, logos, tests), RasQ-LED and Quantum Fractals are on the card from the\nstart; the others download on their first start, which needs a network. Docker demos\ntake 2–4 GB each and need a card of 32 GB or more: Qoffee-Maker, Quantum Mixer, and\nin the development image doQumentation, Qiskit Live, QuBins and the Workshop & Qiskit Server.\n\n## Running demos\n\n- **Desktop:** double-click an icon, or a group's folder and then the demo.\n- **RasQberry menu:** **RasQberry Configuration** (or `sudo raspi-config`) →\n**0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos**. All paths below start there.\n- **Terminal:** `rq_demo_run.sh <id>`, e.g. `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-fractals`\n(ids: [demo list](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/)).\n\nMost demos need a display: a monitor, or [VNC](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/).\nTo stop one, close its window or press Enter or Ctrl+C in it. Docker demos keep\nrunning: **Manage demos** → **Stop Docker demos**. An LED demo still holding the\npanel: **Quantum Demos** → **Stop a running LED demo**.\n\n## No LED panel?\n\nLED demos open the panel on screen, so they work without one.\n\nTo watch it in a browser on another device: **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **LED panel** →\n**LED setup & tests** → **Output Targets**, then open `http://rasqberry.local:8098`.\n\n## IBM Quantum accounts\n\nThe demos run on the built-in simulator, without an account; the online IBM Quantum\nComposer simulates up to four qubits without signing in. For real IBM quantum\ncomputers (some demos offer them), get a free account at [quantum.cloud.ibm.com](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com)\nand save its API key once: **0 RasQberry** → **IBM Quantum account** → **Save my API key**.\nMost IBM Quantum Tutorials are written for real hardware.\n\n## Your own Qiskit programs\n\nDouble-click **My Quantum Programs**: JupyterLab opens in `~/My-Quantum-Programs`\nat Hello World, next to example programs. **File** → **New** → **Notebook** starts\nyour own; keep it in that folder, which A/B updates keep. Python files run with\n`rq_python my_program.py` or in Thonny. **System Info** shows the Qiskit version.\n\n## Entangible\n\n*In the development image.* Build a circuit from printed gate tiles and let a\ncamera (USB webcam or Pi camera) read it: print the\n[tiles and board (A4)](https://github.com/JanLahmann/entangible/releases/latest/download/entangible-print-kit-A4.pdf)\nand point the camera down at the board. Phones on the same network join with the\naddress its window shows (they warn about the certificate once: **Advanced** →\n**Proceed**).\n\n## Manage demos\n\n**Quantum Demos** → **Manage demos**:\n\n- **Download all demos:** everything now, before you go somewhere without a network.\n- **Add demo from catalogue:** reviewed partner demos, pinned to a tested version\n(`rq_demo_add_external.sh --list`).\n- **Update demos**, **Remove a demo** (it downloads again when started),\n**Stop Docker demos**.\n\n## At a booth or in class\n\n- Run **Download all demos** while you have a network.\n- **Workshops & events** → **Demo Loop** plays LED demos and Quantum Fractals one\nafter another (Enter skips, x stops); it can start at login.\n- Open the Pi's own [Wi-Fi hotspot](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/#2-wi-fi-hotspot)\nso visitors' phones can join.\n- The screen doesn't blank (`sudo raspi-config` → **2 Display Options** →\n**Screen Blanking**). **0 RasQberry** → **Desktop Settings** → **Browser at login**\nstops rasqberry.org opening at every start.\n- Visitors stop for Quantum Lights Out, Grokking the Bloch Sphere, Quantum Fractals,\nthe LED demos and the Quantum Coin Game ([more](https://rasqberry.org/workshops/#2-for-a-stand)).\n\n## Learning resources\n\n[IBM Quantum Learning](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning) ·\n[Qiskit documentation](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/) ·\n[IBM Quantum Composer](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/composer) ·\n[Fun with Quantum](https://fun-with-quantum.org)\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/bloch-sphere/\n\n# Bloch Sphere\n\nAn interactive Bloch sphere in your browser: click a gate, watch the qubit move.\nIt is the quickest way to build intuition for what quantum gates actually *do* to\na single qubit.\n\n**Needs:** network on first start · **Start it with:** `rq_demo_run.sh grok-bloch`\n\n## Run it\n\nDouble-click the **Grokking the Bloch Sphere** icon on the desktop, and it opens in\nyour browser. After its first download this version works offline and has tips on what to try and a short\nexplanation.\n\nIt is also under **Applications → RasQberry → Grokking the Bloch Sphere**, or in\n`sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **Learn & code**, which also offers the\nonline version.\n\n## Reading the sphere\n\nEvery pure state of one qubit is a point on the sphere:\n\n- **North pole** — |0⟩, **south pole** — |1⟩\n- **Equator** — superpositions with an equal chance of either\n- **X axis** — |+⟩ and |−⟩ (the Hadamard basis)\n- **Y axis** — |+i⟩ and |−i⟩ (the circular basis)\n\nThe state equation is shown at the top, and the probability of measuring |0⟩\nalongside it. Click gates and watch the vector swing: **X**, **Y** and **Z** turn\nthe state half a revolution about their axis, **H** takes the poles to the\nequator (|0⟩ → (|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2), and **Rx/Ry/Rz** rotate by an angle you choose\n(θ = π/8 or π/12).\n\nStart with X, Z and H before the rotation gates — the effect is easier to see.\nThen try reaching a state you pick in advance.\n\n## What it cannot show\n\nWorth knowing, because it is the honest limit of the picture:\n\n- **One qubit only.** There is no Bloch sphere for two entangled qubits — the\nwhole point of entanglement is that neither qubit has a state of its own.\n- **Pure states only.** No mixed states, so no decoherence or noise.\n\n## Credits and links\n\nBuilt by **James Weaver** (JavaFXpert) — [JavaFXpert/grok-bloch](https://github.com/JavaFXpert/grok-bloch).\n\n- [IBM Quantum Learning: single-qubit gates](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/introduction)\n- [IBM Quantum Composer](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/composer) — build circuits with a Bloch sphere beside them\n- [Bloch sphere on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloch_sphere)\n\n*See the [Demo List](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/) for everything else on the image.*\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/fractals/\n\n# Quantum Fractals\n\nA quantum circuit produces a complex number; that number draws a Julia set. Step\nthe circuit through a rotation and the fractal moves with it — so an abstract\nquantum state becomes something you can watch change.\n\n**Needs:** a display · **Start it with:** `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-fractals`\n\n## Run it\n\nOpen the **Play** folder on the desktop and double-click **Quantum Fractals**; it\nstarts generating straight away and opens the result in your browser.\n\nIt is also under **Applications → RasQberry → Quantum Fractals**, or in\n`sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **Play**.\n\n## How it works\n\nA Julia set is defined by repeating one line:\n\n```\nz(n+1) = z(n)² + c\n```\n\nChange the complex constant `c` and you get a completely different shape. This\ndemo takes `c` from a **quantum state** — the amplitudes of a one-qubit circuit\nare complex numbers, so they can be fed straight in.\n\nEach frame advances the circuit a little, giving a new `c` and a new fractal. Run\n60 of them together and the fractal appears to flow, tracing the qubit's path\naround the Bloch sphere. Each frame shows the circuit that made it alongside its\nfractals, and the finished animation is saved as a GIF.\n\nEverything runs on the local simulator — no IBM Quantum account, no network.\n\n## What you'll see\n\nFrames appear one by one in the browser (the Pi computes each one), then an\nanimated GIF of the whole sequence. Images are written to\n`/tmp/rasqberry-fractals-img/`.\n\nA full 60-frame run takes a while on a Pi. If you want it quicker, the constants\nat the top of `fractals.py` are the ones to change:\n\n| Setting | Default | Does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `frame_resolution` | 200 | image size, in pixels square |\n| `number_of_frames` | 60 | how long the animation is |\n| `GIF_ms_intervals` | 200 | ms per frame (200 = 5 fps) |\n| `julia_iterations` | 100 | fractal detail |\n| `zoom` | 1.0 | how far in |\n\n## Credits and links\n\nCreated by **Wiktor Mazin** and team; ported to RasQberry by **Jan-R. Lahmann**.\n\n- [Visualizing Quantum Computing using Fractals](https://github.com/wmazin/Visualizing-Quantum-Computing-using-fractals) — the original\n- [Creating Fractal Art with Qiskit](https://medium.com/qiskit/creating-fractal-art-with-qiskit-df69427026a0)\n- [Fractal Animations with Quantum Computing on a Raspberry Pi](https://medium.com/qiskit/fractal-animations-with-quantum-computing-on-a-raspberry-pi-8834ef43d423)\n- [Julia sets on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_set)\n\n*See the [Demo List](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/) for everything else on the image.*\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/led-display/\n\n# LED Text & Logo Display\n\nThe model's LED panel (24×8 LEDs) shows qubits, text, logos and color effects.\nNo panel? LED demos open the same view on screen:\n\n## What you can do\n\n- **Show text:** scrolling, static or flashing, in eight colors.\n- **Show logos:** IBM, RasQberry, or your own image.\n- **Run effects:** rainbow, color gradients, a logo slideshow.\n- **See qubits:** RasQ-LED, Quantum Lights Out and Quantum Raspberry Tie measure\ncircuits and light the panel ([all LED demos](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/#2-led-panel)).\n- **Write your own:** a small Python API (below).\n\nAll in `sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **LED panel**.\nThe **LED panel** folder on the desktop has the LED demos.\n\n## Set up your panel first\n\nThe **LED Setup Wizard** finds out how your panel is wired and which way up it\nhangs: it shows the IBM logo in up to four colors, and you pick the one that reads\nupright. It knows the single 24×8 and the quad 4×12 panel; other layouts get a\nstep-by-step walkthrough. **LED panel** → **LED setup & tests** → **LED Setup Wizard**\n(or `sudo rq_led_setup_wizard.sh`); the setup checklist offers a short version.\nThe result, `LED_LAYOUT`, survives [A/B updates](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/03-ab-boot/).\n\n## Text and logos\n\n**LED panel** → **Text & logos**: **Display Custom Text**, **Display Logo from\nLibrary**, and the text, color and logo demos. From a terminal:\n`sudo rq_led_display_text.sh` or `sudo rq_led_display_logo.sh`.\n\nYour own logo: in **Display Logo from Library** (or `sudo rq_led_display_logo.sh`),\nchoose **Browse for custom image...**, then a PNG or JPG. 24×8 pixels fits\nexactly; other sizes are resized. Simple, high-contrast shapes work best. The\nbuilt-in logos are in `/usr/config/LED-Logos`.\n\n## Configuration\n\nSettings are in `/usr/config/rasqberry_environment.env`; change them with\n**0 RasQberry** → **Advanced** → **Edit a RasQberry Two setting**:\n\n- `LED_LAYOUT`: set by the wizard (`single-24x8` by default; also `quad-4x12`,\n`quad-2x2-12x4`, `triple-8x8`, `single-8x32`). The LED count and matrix size\nfollow from it.\n- `LED_DEFAULT_BRIGHTNESS`: 0.0–1.0, default 0.4. Easier: **LED setup & tests** →\n**LED brightness**.\n- `LED_PIXEL_ORDER`: color order of your LEDs (default GRB).\n- `LED_GPIO_PIN`: data pin (default 18).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **Upside down or scrambled:** run the LED Setup Wizard. Static text shows about\nfour characters.\n- **Settings come back after a restart:** `LED_` lines in `rasqberry_boot.env` on\nthe boot drive (bootfs, or BOOT-A on the A/B image) win at every start: put a `#`\nin front or change them there.\n- **Wrong colors:** check `LED_PIXEL_ORDER` (RGB or GRB).\n- **LEDs stay dark:** one program drives the panel at a time. Stop a running demo\n(**Quantum Demos** → **Stop a running LED demo**) or restart, then test with\n`rq_demo_run.sh led-demos led-test`. Check wiring and power: on a Pi 5, LEDs that\nstop after a while mean a weak power supply: RasQberry notices and offers a lower\nbrightness. Better: the official 27 W supply.\n- **Switch the LEDs off:** **Clear All LEDs** in the **LED panel** folder, or\n`sudo rq_clear_leds.sh` (`--stop` also stops a program holding the panel).\n\n## For developers\n\nFour functions are the supported LED API; programs using them keep working with\nlater releases. Everything else in `rq_led_utils` is internal and may change.\n\n```python\nfrom rq_led_utils import get_pixels, matrix_size, set_xy, clear_all_leds\n\npixels = get_pixels(brightness=0.2)  # the LED strip (one shared object)\nwidth, height = matrix_size()        # (24, 8) on the RasQberry panels\nset_xy(pixels, 0, 0, (255, 0, 0))    # top-left LED red\nset_xy(pixels, width - 1, height - 1, (0, 0, 255))  # bottom-right blue\npixels.show()                        # nothing changes before show()\nclear_all_leds()\n```\n\n- x runs from 0 (left) to width-1, y from 0 (top) to height-1. `set_xy` follows\n`LED_LAYOUT`, so one program works on both kits; positions off the panel are\nignored. Avoid `pixels[number]`: raw numbers follow the wiring, not rows.\n- Run it with `rq_python my_program.py`. On a Pi 5, Thonny and `python3` in the\nRasQberry environment work too; a Pi 4 needs `rq_python` (the LED driver needs\nroot there). Never `sudo python3`.\n- Examples: `~/My-Quantum-Programs/03_led_hello.py` and `04_bell_on_leds.py`. The\nmenu's demos are `/usr/bin/demo_led_*.py`, e.g. `rq_python /usr/bin/demo_led_ibm_logo.py`.\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/qoffee-maker/\n\n# Qoffee-Maker\n\nBuild a quantum circuit, measure it, and get the drink the measurement chose.\nEach beverage is a bit pattern — 000, 001, 010 … — so ordering a cappuccino means\nconstructing a circuit whose measurement lands on the right one. Quantum\nmechanics being what it is, you can shift the odds but never quite give an order.\n\n**Needs:** a display · a network connection · a 32 GB card · **Start it with:** `rq_demo_run.sh qoffee-maker`\n\n## Run it\n\nOpen the **Big projects** folder on the desktop and double-click **Qoffee-Maker**.\nIt starts the Qoffee\ncontainer, opens `qoffee.ipynb`, and switches straight into the app view — you do\nnot have to run any cells yourself.\n\nIt is also under **Applications → RasQberry → Qoffee-Maker**, or in\n`sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **Big projects**.\n\nThe first launch pulls the Qoffee container image, so give it a few minutes and a\ndecent connection. After that it starts quickly.\n\n**If you end up looking at the notebook instead of the app** — the auto-start did\nnot take, or you opened `qoffee.ipynb` yourself — click the **rocket icon** (🚀)\nin the toolbar. That runs every cell and starts the app view, which is exactly\nwhat the icon does for you.\n\n## What you'll see\n\nDrag gates onto the qubits to build a circuit. The interface shows the\nprobability of each of the eight outcomes, and which drink each one pours. Choose\na backend to compare an ideal simulation against one with realistic noise — the\nnoisy run is the honest one, and it is a good way to feel what error means in\npractice before anyone mentions error correction.\n\nStart by aiming for an outcome you can hit reliably (000, or 111), then try for a\nspecific drink in the middle. That is where it gets interesting: superposition\nmeans your circuit sets *probabilities*, so you may need a few attempts — which is\nthe point of the demo.\n\nWith a Home Connect coffee machine, the winning measurement actually brews it:\nregister an application as in the\n[Qoffee-Maker README](https://github.com/JanLahmann/Qoffee-Maker#install-home-connect),\nthen put its client ID and secret, and `HOMECONNECT_API_URL=https://api.home-connect.com/`,\ninto `~/RasQberry-Two/demos/Qoffee-Maker/.env`. Without one, everything works\nexcept the coffee.\n\n## Credits and documentation\n\nBy **Max Simon** and **Jan-R. Lahmann**, from the \"serious games for quantum\ncomputing\" family. Full details, the hardware setup and the event kit are on the\nproject's own site:\n\n- **[qoffee-maker.org](https://qoffee-maker.org)** — the project and its documentation\n- [Fun with Quantum](https://fun-with-quantum.org) — more quantum games (the Quantum Coin Game is on this image)\n- [IBM Quantum Learning: measurement](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/quantum-information)\n\n*See the [Demo List](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/) for everything else on the image.*\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/quantum-lights-out/\n\n# Quantum Lights Out\n\nThe [Lights Out puzzle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lights_Out_(game)) — press a\ntile, it toggles itself and its neighbors, and you have to turn every light off —\nsolved by **Grover's search** running on a quantum simulator, with each step of\nthe solution played out on the LED panel.\n\n**Needs:** the LED panel or its on-screen view · network on first start · **Start it with:** `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-lights-out`\n\n## Run it\n\nOpen the **LED panel** folder on the desktop and double-click **Quantum Lights\nOut**; the puzzle and its solution appear on the LED panel (without a panel, in its\n[on-screen view](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/00-overview/#2-no-led-panel)).\n\nIt is also under **Applications → RasQberry → Quantum Lights Out**, or in\n`sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **LED panel** →\n**Quantum Lights Out**, where **Run Demo (console)** plays in the terminal instead\n(`rq_demo_run.sh quantum-lights-out console`).\n\n## What you'll see\n\nA 3×3 grid of lit tiles appears in the middle of the panel. After a short pause\nwhile the circuit runs, each press of the solution is shown in turn, until the\ngrid goes dark.\n\n## How it works\n\nLights Out is a **search** problem: of all the combinations of presses, which one\nturns everything off? That is what Grover's algorithm is for.\n\nThe circuit holds the board, a set of \"flip\" qubits standing for the presses you\nmight make, and an **oracle** — a piece of circuit that recognizes a winning\ncombination without being told what it is. Grover then alternates the oracle with\na **diffusion** step, and each round nudges probability toward the answers. After\nenough rounds, measuring the flip qubits gives you a solution.\n\nThat is the interesting part: the oracle only *checks* a combination, yet Grover\nfinds the one that works in noticeably fewer tries than testing them one by one.\n\nThe quantum code comes from the **IBM Quantum Challenge 2020**, and runs on the\nlocal Aer simulator — no IBM Quantum account, no network.\n\n## Credits and links\n\nBuilt by **Luka Dojcinovic** — [Luka-D/Quantum-Lights-Out](https://github.com/Luka-D/Quantum-Lights-Out),\nwith the quantum algorithm from the IBM Quantum Challenge 2020.\n\n- [Lights Out on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lights_Out_(game))\n- [IBM Quantum Learning: Grover's algorithm](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/grover-algorithm/introduction)\n\n*See the [Demo List](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/) for everything else on the image.*\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/raspberry-tie/\n\n# Quantum Raspberry Tie\n\nRun a quantum circuit — on a simulator, or on real IBM Quantum hardware in the\n[development image](https://rasqberry.org/latest/dev/) — and watch\nthe measured qubits light up on the panel, laid out the way the qubits are\nactually arranged on the processor.\n\n**Needs:** the LED panel or its on-screen view · a display · network on first start · IBM Quantum account optional\n**Start it with:** `rq_demo_run.sh quantum-raspberry-tie`\n\n## Run it\n\nOpen the **LED panel** folder on the desktop and double-click **Quantum Raspberry\nTie**.\n\nIt is also under **Applications → RasQberry → Quantum Raspberry Tie**, or in\n`sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Quantum Demos** → **LED panel**.\n\nFrom a terminal you can pick the backend:\n\n```bash\nrq_demo_run.sh quantum-raspberry-tie            # local Aer simulator (default)\nrq_demo_run.sh quantum-raspberry-tie noise      # simulator with a noise model\nrq_demo_run.sh quantum-raspberry-tie real       # real IBM Quantum hardware (development image)\n```\n\nThe `real` variant is in the [development image](https://rasqberry.org/latest/dev/) only. It needs a network connection and your IBM Quantum account, and\nyour job may sit in a queue before it runs.\n\n**Your IBM Quantum account** (development image): the `real` variant asks for your API key when none\nis saved, and saves it. Or save it once: `sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** →\n**IBM Quantum account** → **Save my API key**.\n\n## What you'll see\n\nEach qubit is a colored pixel: **blue** for a qubit measured as |1⟩, **red** for\n|0⟩, and a paler shade for qubits that were not measured. The pixels are not in a\nline — they are placed to match the connectivity of a real IBM processor, so a\n5-qubit run makes the \"bowtie\", and larger runs the heavy-hex pattern used by\ncurrent devices.\n\nComparing the same circuit on the simulator and on real hardware is the thing\nworth doing: the ideal result is crisp, and the real device shows you noise.\n\nNo LEDs? It also renders to an SVG you can open in a browser.\n\n## Credits and documentation\n\nWritten and maintained by **Kevin Roche**. The upstream project documents the\ndisplay modes, backends and options in full — start there rather than here:\n\n- **[KPRoche/quantum-raspberry-tie](https://github.com/KPRoche/quantum-raspberry-tie)** — the project and its documentation\n- [IBM Quantum Platform](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/) — for a free account and API key, if you want the `real` variant\n- [Bill of Materials](https://rasqberry.org/01-3d-model/01-bill-of-materials/) · [Hardware Assembly Guide](https://rasqberry.org/01-3d-model/02-hardware-assembly-guide/) — for the LED panel\n\n*See the [Demo List](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/01-demo-list/) for everything else on the image.*\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/02-software/01-installation-overview/\n\n# RasQberry Installation Overview\n\nWrite RasQberry Two to an SD card, start your Pi, and keep it up to date.\n\n## What you need\n\n- **Raspberry Pi 5** (recommended, 2 GB RAM is enough) or **Raspberry Pi 4B** (4 GB RAM).\n- **MicroSD card:** 128 GB high-speed (A2, U3) recommended, 16 GB minimum.\n- **Official power supply:** 27 W USB-C for the Pi 5, 15 W for the Pi 4. Ordinary\nchargers are too weak for a bright LED panel. An active cooler for the Pi 5.\n- **Internet:** most demos download on their first start.\n- Optional: the LED panel, a display, keyboard and mouse. Without a display, use\n[VNC](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/).\n\nAll parts, including the 3D-printed case: [bill of materials](https://rasqberry.org/01-3d-model/01-bill-of-materials/).\n\n## Which image?\n\nPick **RasQberry Two Beta**, whatever your card. From 64 GB it holds two systems:\nreleases install over the air and you can always go back ([A/B image](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/03-ab-boot/)).\n**RasQberry Two Beta — single system** is always one system.\n\n| Card | What fits |\n|---|---|\n| **128 GB (recommended)** | Two systems, all Docker demos |\n| 64 GB | Two systems, some Docker demos |\n| 32 GB | One system, with the Docker demos |\n| 16 GB | One system, without the Docker demos |\n\nDocker demos (Qoffee-Maker, Quantum Mixer, Qiskit Live, Workshop & Qiskit Server)\ntake 2–4 GB each, per system.\n\n## Write the card\n\nInstall [Raspberry Pi Imager](https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/) 2.0.3 or newer, then:\n\n[▶ Write RasQberry Two to your SD card](rpi-imager://open?repo=https://RasQberry.org/RQB-images.json)\n\n1. Let your browser open Imager and confirm **Switch repository**.\n2. **Device:** your Pi. **OS:** **RasQberry Two Beta** (untested builds are under\n**RasQberry developer builds**). **Storage:** your card; writing erases it.\n3. **Customisation** (optional, below), then **Write**. Put the card in the Pi and switch it on.\n\n### Customisation\n\nImager can prepare the Pi so it needs no screen:\n\n- **Localisation:** time zone and keyboard (also sets the Wi-Fi country).\n- **Wi-Fi:** your network and password.\n- **User:** your login name and password instead of `rasqberry` / `Qiskit1!`. If the\nname can't be used (the system already has it), the login stays `rasqberry` and the\nfirst login says why.\n- **Remote access:** SSH is on. **Use public key authentication** accepts only\nyour key and needs the **User** step.\n- **Hostname:** the Pi becomes `<name>.local` instead of `rasqberry.local`.\n\nCustomisation applies to the first start of a newly written card only.\n\n### If the link does not open Imager\n\n- **Add the repository by hand:** in Imager, **App Options** → **Content Repository**\n→ **Use custom URL** → `https://RasQberry.org/RQB-images.json` → **Apply & Restart**.\n- **Start Imager from a terminal:**\n\n  ```bash\n# macOS\n/Applications/Raspberry\\ Pi\\ Imager.app/Contents/MacOS/rpi-imager --repo https://RasQberry.org/RQB-images.json\n\n# Windows\n\"C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Raspberry Pi Imager\\rpi-imager.exe\" --repo https://RasQberry.org/RQB-images.json\n  ```\n\n- **Download the image** from [rasqberry.org/latest/](https://rasqberry.org/latest/) and write it with\n**Choose OS** → **Use custom**. Imager then offers no customisation.\n\n## First start\n\nThe first start takes a few minutes and the Pi restarts on its own, sometimes more\nthan once: don't unplug it. Then\nthe desktop opens with the setup checklist. Over SSH or VNC, log in with the user\nfrom Imager; without one, see [first access without a screen](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/#3-first-access-without-a-screen).\n\n### Setup checklist\n\nThe checklist offers what still needs you:\n\n- **Keyboard and time zone** (UK until you change them).\n- **Wi-Fi**, when the Pi has no network.\n- **Your own password.** Keep `Qiskit1!` only for a booth.\n- **A name for this RasQberry**, when several share a network.\n- **The LED panel:** pick the color in which the IBM logo reads upright. No panel?\nLED demos then show on screen.\n- **Download all demos** now, or each on its first start.\n- **Touch mode**, for a touchscreen.\n\nEvery step can wait: the **RasQberry Setup** icon opens the list again. After\n**Don't show again** it stays in **0 RasQberry** → **Setup Checklist**.\n\n## The RasQberry menu\n\nDemo icons and group folders are on the desktop. Everything else is in the menu:\ndouble-click **RasQberry Configuration** (or run `sudo raspi-config`), then **0 RasQberry**.\n\n- **Quantum Demos:** learning paths, demo groups, download, update or remove demos.\n- **Desktop Settings:** touch mode, browser at login, Bluetooth sound delay.\n- **IBM Quantum account:** save your API key for real quantum hardware.\n- **Remote Access & Security:** password, SSH, VNC, the Pi's name, Raspberry Pi Connect.\n- **Wi-Fi Hotspot:** the Pi's own network for booths and classrooms.\n- **Software & Image Updates:** newer images; the Slot Manager on the A/B image.\n- **System Info:** versions of the image, Python and Qiskit.\n- **Advanced:** edit a setting, refresh the demo list, update from a GitHub branch.\n- **Shut Down Safely:** LEDs off, then shutdown; switch the power off once the\ngreen light stays off.\n\nThe image runs Raspberry Pi OS Trixie (64-bit) with Qiskit 2.x in\n`~/RasQberry-Two/venv/RQB2` (`source ~/RasQberry-Two/venv/RQB2/bin/activate`). Code for Qiskit 1.x may need the\n[migration guide](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/migration-guides/qiskit-2.0).\n\n## Keeping up to date\n\n- **System updates:** the update icon in the taskbar, or\n`sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade`.\n- **New RasQberry releases:** on the A/B image, install into the other system\n([how](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/03-ab-boot/)). Otherwise write a new card; copy your\nnotebooks and `~/.qiskit` to a USB stick first.\n- A daily check tells you when a newer release is out.\n\n## Downloads\n\n- [rasqberry.org/latest/](https://rasqberry.org/latest/): all current images. Direct links:\n[beta](https://rasqberry.org/latest/beta), [dev](https://rasqberry.org/latest/dev).\n- [GitHub Releases](https://github.com/JanLahmann/RasQberry-Two/releases): every build with release notes.\n- For scripts: [RQB-images.json](https://rasqberry.org/RQB-images.json), [RQB-releases.json](https://rasqberry.org/RQB-releases.json)\n(with checksums), [RQB-images-all.json](https://rasqberry.org/RQB-images-all.json).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **Can't find the Pi:** try `rasqberry.local`. The LED panel scrolls the IP\naddress at start-up; **System Info** shows it too.\n- **VNC doesn't connect:** VNC stays off until you set your own password\n([remote access](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/)).\n- **\"Failed to run demo\":** the message names the cause, usually no network on a\nfirst start, or a display demo started over SSH (start it on the desktop or\nover VNC).\n- **Disk full:** Docker demos need 32 GB or more ([card sizes](#2-which-image)).\n- **LED panel stays dark:** [LED troubleshooting](https://rasqberry.org/03-quantum-computing-demos/led-display/).\n- **Still stuck?** `rq_info.sh --report` saves a report in your home folder. Attach\nit to a [GitHub issue](https://github.com/JanLahmann/RasQberry-Two/issues) or\ne-mail it to info@rasqberry.org.\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/02-software/02-system-options/\n\n# System Options\n\nConnect your RasQberry, open its own Wi-Fi for a booth or class, reach it from your\ncomputer, and add sound. Settings are in `sudo raspi-config` (or double-click\n**RasQberry Configuration**); password, SSH, VNC and the Pi's name also in\n**0 RasQberry** → **Remote Access & Security**. Raspberry Pi Imager can set Wi-Fi,\na user and an SSH key before the first start\n([customisation](https://rasqberry.org/02-software/01-installation-overview/#3-customisation)).\n\n## Wi-Fi\n\nNo network at the first start? The setup checklist offers Wi-Fi. Later: the network\nicon in the taskbar, or `sudo raspi-config` → **1 System Options** → **S1 Wireless LAN**.\nThe Pi remembers its networks and reconnects by itself.\n\n**No screen and no network?** A new Pi that has never had a network opens the Wi-Fi\n**RasQberry-Setup-XXXX** after about two minutes, password `rasqberry` (an LED panel\nscrolls both). Join it with your phone: a page lists the networks in range; pick\nyours. It closes after 30 minutes unused (an hour at most); switch the Pi off and on\nto open it again.\n\nThe Pi's IP address: **System Info** in **0 RasQberry**, `hostname -I`, or the LED\npanel at start-up.\n\n## Wi-Fi hotspot\n\nThe Pi's own Wi-Fi, for booths and classrooms where the venue's Wi-Fi keeps devices\napart. Phones and laptops join it and open the demos at `http://10.42.0.1`.\n\n- **Start it:** **0 RasQberry** → **Wi-Fi Hotspot** → **Hotspot**.\n- **Let people join:** **Join card** opens a page with the name, password and QR\ncodes, to show or print. Over SSH, **Show name, password and QR codes** prints them.\n- **Name and password:** `RasQberry-XXXX` and a random password, unless you set your own.\n- **Start at boot**, **share this Pi's internet**, and the **band** (5 GHz; 2.4 GHz\nfor older devices) are in the same menu.\n- **One Wi-Fi can't do both:** the hotspot takes a USB Wi-Fi adapter if there is one.\nOtherwise the built-in Wi-Fi leaves your network while the hotspot runs; a network\ncable keeps the Pi online.\n- **SSH and VNC from the hotspot:** by default only once no account that can use\n`sudo` still has `Qiskit1!`, because the hotspot's password is on a public card.\n**SSH/VNC from the hotspot** sets it to always or never.\n- **Before the first start:** a file `rasqberry-hotspot.txt` on the card's boot\npartition (the CONFIG partition on the A/B image) with lines such as\n`HOTSPOT_SSID=Booth`, `HOTSPOT_PASSWORD=…`, `HOTSPOT_AT_BOOT=yes` sets the hotspot up.\n\n## Remote access\n\nOn most networks the Pi is `rasqberry.local`; otherwise use its IP address. Log in\nwith the user from Imager, otherwise `rasqberry` / `Qiskit1!`.\n\n### First access without a screen\n\nWithout a user or SSH key from Imager, the Pi protects its published password:\n\n1. **Log in over SSH within 15 minutes:** `ssh rasqberry@rasqberry.local`, password\n`Qiskit1!`. The 15 minutes start when the Pi is on the network; on the very first\nstart, wait for its restart. Missed it? Switch the Pi off and on.\n2. **Set your own password.** The Pi asks right away: `Qiskit1!`, then the new\npassword twice. It is also your password for VNC and `sudo`.\n3. **VNC comes on** once the password is changed.\n\nWith a screen, change the password in the setup checklist instead. To change the\nrules, edit `rasqberry_boot.env` on the boot drive (bootfs; BOOT-A or BOOT-B on the\nA/B image): remove the `#` and set a value. `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_MIN=30` gives 30\nminutes (`0`: keys only); `DEFAULT_PW_WINDOW_VNC=yes` turns VNC on as well, for a\nclassroom or booth.\n\n### SSH\n\nSSH is on: `ssh rasqberry@rasqberry.local`, or `ssh <user>@rasqberry.local` with\nyour own user. Once you have your own password, `ssh-copy-id <user>@rasqberry.local`\nlets you log in with your key.\n\n### VNC (the desktop)\n\nVNC comes on once the Pi has your own password. Use **RealVNC Viewer** or\n**TigerVNC** and connect to `rasqberry.local`; the Mac's Screen Sharing, TightVNC\nand UltraVNC can't connect. Give the Pi a minute after switching on. Switched off in\n**3 Interface Options** → **VNC**, it stays off.\n\nStart display demos on the desktop, locally or over VNC: over SSH they have no screen.\n\n### Raspberry Pi Connect\n\nThe desktop and a terminal in your browser, from anywhere: the Connect icon in the\ntaskbar → **Turn On Raspberry Pi Connect** → **Sign In** (a free Raspberry Pi ID),\nthen open [connect.raspberrypi.com](https://connect.raspberrypi.com). Imager can sign\nit in before the first start.\n\n## Sound\n\nSound plays over HDMI or a Bluetooth speaker (Bluetooth icon → **Add Device**;\nright-click the volume icon to pick the output). A Bluetooth speaker plays late:\n**0 RasQberry** → **Desktop Settings** → **Sound delay** → **Calibrate** plays ticks\nwith flashes on the LED panel; adjust until they come together.\n\n## Password\n\nEveryone who reads this website knows `Qiskit1!`. On a shared network, set your own:\nin the setup checklist, with `passwd`, or **1 System Options** → **S3 Password**.\nKeep it only for a booth (then see [first access without a screen](#3-first-access-without-a-screen)).\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n\n### Page: https://rasqberry.org/02-software/03-ab-boot/\n\n# A/B Image: Two Systems, One Card\n\nThe A/B image is the recommended RasQberry Two image (**RasQberry Two Beta** in\nRaspberry Pi Imager). On a card of 64 GB or more it holds **two systems**, Slot A\nand Slot B. **The SD card can stay in the Pi for good:** new releases install\nover the air, each into the system you are not running, while the running one\nstays as it is, so a failed update never leaves you without a working Pi.\n\n## Card sizes\n\n| Card | What you get |\n|---|---|\n| **128 GB, A2/U3 (recommended)** | Two systems, each with room for all Docker demos, and a shared data partition (10% of the card) |\n| 64 GB | Two systems (about 28 GB each) and the shared data partition. Room for some Docker demos |\n| 16 GB or 32 GB | One system that uses the whole card (Docker demos need 32 GB). There are no updates in place: write a new card for each release |\n\nThe standard image (**RasQberry Two Beta — single system**) is still published. It\nis always one system, like the A/B image on a small card.\n\n## First start\n\nThe first start prepares the card. It takes a few minutes and the Pi restarts\non its own: do not unplug it. It is done when the desktop appears.\n\nOn a card of 64 GB or more, the card is split into the two systems\nautomatically. To keep it as one system, create an empty file named\n`no-auto-expand` on the **CONFIG** drive before the first start.\n\n## Update\n\n1. `sudo raspi-config` → **0 RasQberry** → **Software & Image Updates** →\n**Check for a newer image** tells you whether a new release is out. A\nterminal or SSH login also shows a one-line notice.\n2. **Slot Manager** → **Install an update into the other system**. It downloads\nabout 2 GB and takes 10–20 minutes, then the Pi restarts into the new system.\nIf nothing newer is out, it offers your release again as a second copy, and\n**Other release...** for another one.\n\n3. If the update works, the new system becomes the one the Pi starts from. If\nit doesn't, the Pi goes back to the other one by itself.\n\nUpdates take turns between Slot A and Slot B. The slot you left keeps the\nprevious system, so you can go back to it.\n\nRasQberry warns you before a downgrade (an older release, or a development\nbuild over a beta), and before it replaces your only beta or stable system.\nThen it suggests the safer way: switch to that system first and install the\nupdate into the other slot.\n\n## What an update keeps\n\n- the **Shared** and **My-Quantum-Programs** folders in your home folder\n- your IBM Quantum account (`~/.qiskit`)\n- Wi-Fi networks and LED panel settings\n- your password, the Pi's name, time zone, language and keyboard\n- SSH keys, so remote logins keep working\n\nEach system has its own demos: after an update they download again on their\nfirst start, Docker demos included (2–4 GB each). Other files in your home folder\nstay in the other slot: switch back to fetch them.\n\n## Go back\n\n- **Use the other system:** **Slot Manager** → **Switch to Slot A** (or B).\n- **The screen stays black after an update:** switch the Pi off and on again.\nIt starts the previous system.\n- **Still nothing on the screen:** put the card in a computer and open the\n**CONFIG** drive. In `autoboot.txt`, under `[all]`, set `boot_partition=2`\n(Slot A), save, and put the card back.\n\n**System Info** in the RasQberry menu, or **Slot Manager** → **Show slot details**,\nshows what each slot holds and which one you are running.\n\n## Raspberry Pi 4\n\nThe A/B image needs a recent bootloader. If a Pi 4 does not start from the\ncard, update its bootloader with Raspberry Pi Imager (**Misc utility images** →\n**Bootloader** → **SD Card Boot**), then try again.\n\n## The slot badge\n\nThe taskbar shows which system runs: **A** or **B**, green when confirmed, amber\nwhile a new system is on trial, a red **!** when an update failed and the Pi went\nback. A dot means a newer release is out (a new beta a few days after its\nrelease). Click the badge for both slots, **System Info**, **Software & Image\nUpdates** and **What's new**. After an update, What's new opens once by itself.\n\n## Details\n\nThe partition layout, the boot mechanism and the command-line tools are in\n[docs/ab-boot.md](https://github.com/JanLahmann/RasQberry-Two/blob/development/docs/ab-boot.md).\n\nQuestions? Ask [Ket](https://rasqberry.org/?assistant=1).\n";
export const DEMO_IDS = ["led-demos","rasq-led","quantum-lights-out","quantum-raspberry-tie","led-painter","fun-with-quantum","quantum-paradoxes","quantum-fractals","qoffee-maker","quantum-mixer","entangible","grok-bloch","grok-bloch-web","qiskit-tutorials","quantum-lab","qubins","ibm-tutorials","ibm-courses","composer","doqumentation"];
